  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Logan Webb was a shutout, and that early lead held through nine innings. The offense did not need to do much. Just enough to finish what the starter built. Rafael Devers supplied the power with two home runs, and the bullpen closed without drama. This is what happens when pitching dominates: the game becomes simple.",
    "what_this_means": "Webb's line was the real story. Seven innings, one hit, zero runs, six strikeouts. That is dominance disguised as routine. The Giants broke through twice early, in the second and third innings, and the rest was mop-up. Devers' two-run shots gave the team more than enough cushion. The bullpen held the space they were given: Walker and Hentges combined for two clean innings. This was not a fight. It was a controlled finish built on a starter who did not allow room for doubt. Eight strikeouts by the lineup said nothing went easy, but nothing had to. The game was decided before most teams would have found their rhythm.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is a fragile team that won decisively, and that matters. Inconsistency has been the problem all season. Hot one night, cold the next, no middle. Today the pitching was elite and the offense was adequate, and the two added up to a shutout. That alignment does not happen often. Webb gave them the game. The rest did not waste it.",
